{"report": "The 10-year breakeven has risen to 2.28% from 2.17% at the oldest observation in this series, a gain of +0.11 pp. Over the near term (21 days), the breakeven has drifted down by 0.08 pp from what was likely a local high around 2.36%. This pullback follows a move above the recent 12-month high of 2.49%, so some retracement is unsurprising after an overshoot. The current level of 2.28% sits 0.28 pp above the Fed's 2% target and at the 40th percentile of the 12-month range, well within recent bounds. The breakeven has not approached the 2.43% threshold I identified as a falsifier, and the Fed language remains hawkish: no rate-cut signals, continued QT, and emphasis on "greater confidence" in sustainable disinflation before cuts begin.

